WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.210
It might be strange to remember now, but there
was a time when the summer box office wasn't

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.140
dominated by comic book movies.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.180
How far back can you trace the origins of
our current superheroic movie landscape?

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.660
It just might start in the year 2000, when
X-Men changed everything.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.520
Looking at the opening sequence of a film
like 1997's Batman &amp; Robin, it's the hero

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.660
who's immediately set up as the main character.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:25.940
In this case, it's both Batman and Robin.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.420
"Chicks dig the car.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:29.880
This is why Superman works alone."

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.640
Everything about it is cheesy and over the
top, and then X-Men came along and did something

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.820
completely different.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:41.480
The film opens in a Nazi concentration camp
in 1944 with a boy who, separated from his

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:45.430
parents, makes a desperate attempt to reopen
the camp's metal gates with his superhuman

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:46.040
powers.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.340
The boy is a young Erik Lehnsherr, who would
go on to become the film's primary antagonist,

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.000
Magneto.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.100
But we don't see him as a villain - at least
not at first.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.560
We see him as a child subjected to an unimaginable
horror.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.100
When it is revealed that the child has grown
into the villain, it's as a sympathetic one.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.420
Fast forward to the present, and it's clear
that the trend of creating sympathetic villains

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:08.460
hasn't gone away.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.180
X-Men played a huge role in introducing the
idea of a villainous monster you could actually

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:13.980
care about.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.820
This also blurred the line between good and
evil.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.591
While Professor X advocates for peace, Magneto
is convinced the only outcome is war, but

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:27.680
ultimately, they both want the same thing:
for mutantkind to be raised up from oppression.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.260
In his review of the film, Roger Ebert said:

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.120
"X-Men is at least not a manic editing frenzy
for atrophied attention spans.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.180
It's restrained and introspective for a superhero
epic…"

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.800
In fact, much of X-Men's hour and 44 minute
runtime is spent on character development,

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:49.720
introducing audiences to the main players,
explaining their special abilities, and delving

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.860
into their personal struggles against bigotry
and discrimination.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.239
Fight scenes and action sequences are there,
but they're not the main focus of the film.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.500
"So read my mind."

00:01:58.780 --> 00:01:59.700
"I'd rather not."

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.720
"Come on, you afraid you might like it?"

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:07.120
It might have something to do with the fact
that director Bryan Singer had never read

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.240
the comics as a kid.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:12.780
Singer told the Los Angeles Times in 2016
that it wasn't until he started watching the

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.920
animated series later on in life that he began
to form an attachment to the characters.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.160
He explained:

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.280
"When I suddenly come across this universe
that's all about misfits and outcasts who

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.480
become superheroes, it intrigued me.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:29.860
I started to figure a way to tell this story
not just to the fan base but to a wider audience."

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:34.310
The focus of the film became a social message,
as opposed to the "big stuff" audiences had

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.540
come to expect from action blockbusters.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.300
Magneto wasn't the only X-Men character to
get his on-screen introduction via a relatable,

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.200
human backstory.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:49.310
In Mississippi, we see 17-year-old Marie accidentally
put her boyfriend into a coma after she kisses

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:50.420
him for the first time.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:56.080
Regardless of the continuity between her portrayal
in the original comics and in the 2000 film,

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.020
Marie - later Rogue - wasn't introduced as
a fully-fledged misfit superhero.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.400
She was our point-of-view character, a regular
person, with not-so-regular problems.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.999
Bryan Singer told the Los Angeles Times that
he treated the characters in X-Men with, quote

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.520
"the same seriousness" as those in The Usual
Suspects.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:19.400
That legitimacy was crucial to making a satisfying
movie, he explained, saying:

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:23.469
"You can't just take a bunch of characters
and throw them together and stir them up in

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.230
a soup and hope that it works."

00:03:26.519 --> 00:03:31.060
From the beginning, X-Men worked to set itself
apart from its comic movie predecessors via

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.100
its tone and approach to storytelling.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:38.340
Bryan Singer went into the movie from a standpoint
of really wanting it to be a serious film

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:43.239
set in a fantastic world, but he was also
keenly aware of how audiences would feel going

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.100
into a so-called "comic movie."

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:50.030
In an interview with MTV News, Hugh Jackman,
who played Wolverine throughout the franchise,

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:54.340
said that Singer was so dedicated to making
X-Men stand out as more than just a comic

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:59.099
film that he wouldn't even allow comic books
onto the set, thereby effectively making source

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.349
material contraband.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.500
"Hey, where are all my comic books?”

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.380
“Oh, those dreadful things?

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:06.880
I gave those away."

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.620
Jackman went on to explain:

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.380
"He really wanted to take comic book characters
seriously as real, three-dimensional characters.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:16.300
People who don't understand these comics might
think they're two-dimensional."

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:22.039
The three biggest films of the 2000 summer
box office were headlined by Tom Cruise, Russell

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:24.300
Crowe, and George Clooney, respectively.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.840
At that point in time, all three had proven
themselves as leading men.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:33.460
X-Men's stars were a couple of actors who
were in their '60s -- a pre-Gandalf Ian McKellen,

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:38.580
and Patrick Stewart, then best known for telling
Wesley Crusher to shut up in Star Trek: TNG.

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.060
“Shut up, Wesley!”

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:45.599
The two leads were supported by a mostly unknown
cast who had done some TV work and a teen

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.040
horror flick here and there.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:51.200
Halle Berry had done some high-profile movie
work, but she wouldn't go on to win her Oscar

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.340
for Monster's Ball or become a box office
force of her own for a couple more years.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:59.780
Speaking with MTV News, Hugh Jackman said
he went into the audition as if it weren't

00:04:59.789 --> 00:05:04.390
a real one, because he thought Dougray Scott
was already going to play the role of Wolverine.

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:08.660
"I think I was sent up there just to kind
of send a message back to Mission: Impossible

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:12.220
II like, you better hurry up because we're
going to start looking at other people.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:13.640
I didn't think it was a real audition."

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.820
What X-Men proved, however, is that you didn't
need a big star to sell a film to audiences.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.420
Endgame marked a high for Marvel Studios,
and for the comic film genre, but chances

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:28.240
are it wouldn't exist had X-Men not first
paved the way.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.970
In 1996, Marvel filed for bankruptcy and sold
off the cinematic rights to several of its

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.020
properties in an effort to stay afloat.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.919
20th Century Fox picked up the rights to the
X-Men, and although the movie was a success

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.360
at the box office, Marvel wound up with zero
to show for it.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:49.050
Two years later, the same would happen with
Sony's Spider-Man, and again, Marvel would

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.360
walk away with nothing.

00:05:50.629 --> 00:05:56.620
In 2005, after X-Men and Spider-Man had proven
how successful a comic film could be if done

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:01.330
properly, Marvel gambled everything they had
to launch their own production studio, with

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:04.850
Iron Man ushering in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe three years later.

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.889
Love it or hate it, X-Men was built to begin
a franchise, and it made major use of a cliffhanger

00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:13.000
after the film's storyline was wrapped up.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:17.960
It's a common thing now, but back then films
didn't typically rely on such gimmicks.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:23.210
The ending of the film sees Professor X visits
Magneto in his plastic cell, where Magneto

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:25.280
shares his plan to continue his fight.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:29.600
It's obvious this isn't the real end of the
story and that there's a definitive plan for

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:30.100
more.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:33.860
And now, we watch comic films and actually
expect a tease for more.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:36.340
“I'm here to talk to you about the Avenger
Initiative."

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:42.940
By the time X-Men arrived in theaters, it
had been over a decade since Tim Burton's

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:45.660
first Batman movie had set the box office
on fire.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:51.060
In the time since, superhero movies had hardly
been shown to be a guaranteed path to blockbuster

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:51.560
success.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:55.420
Even Batman's sequels had turned into a game
of diminishing returns.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.600
"What killed the dinosaurs?

00:06:58.060 --> 00:06:59.480
The ICE AGE!"

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:04.700
2000's X-Men proved two things: the first
is that comic book adaptations didn't have

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:05.480
to be campy.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:08.720
It also proved that comic films could be profitable.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.180
Longtime X-Men comics writer Chris Claremont
told Polygon in 2019:

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:17.180
"Up until then everybody had looked on superhero
films as losers.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:22.500
Then, the X-Men rolled in with a nine-figure
opening weekend, which nobody saw coming…

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:27.240
20 years later we are in the world of today
with Black Panther pulling in three Oscars

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.840
and Into the Spider-Verse getting Best Animated
Film."

